% === provenance: Gamma1994DesignPatterns ===
% CLAIM: The design rule "favor object composition over class
%   inheritance" is stated in Gamma, Helm, Johnson and Vlissides,
%   Design Patterns (1994), and that book is the source the field
%   attributes it to.
% FOUND-VIA: known-item attribution, not discovery.  The session's
%   topic queries (classes-komposition, S1 '("composition over
%   inheritance" OR "favor composition" ...)') return papers that USE
%   the phrase, not the book (books are not indexed).  WebSearch 'Gamma
%   Helm Johnson Vlissides Design Patterns "Favor object composition
%   over class inheritance" chapter 1 sample pdf informit OR pearson'
%   (2026-09-03) found no publisher sample chapter, only the InformIT and
%   Pearson catalogue pages and unauthorised uploads, which were not
%   used.  WebFetch https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Composition_over_inheritance
%   (2026-09-03): the article cites the book, p. 20, for the principle.
% PICKED: the book itself is the origin the field names, so no
%   alternative competes for the attribution; a peer-reviewed paper in
%   the session (Seiter, Palsberg and Lieberherr 1998, tagged
%   gof-reference by the classifier) also refers to it for the rule.
% QUOTE: NOT VERIFIED.  The sentence commonly reproduced -- "Favor
%   object composition over class inheritance." (section 1.6, p. 20) --
%   is quoted here from secondary sources (the Wikipedia article's
%   reference to p. 20) and must not be cited as verbatim on this
%   entry's authority.
% VERIFIED: NOT OBTAINED.  The book is not open access and no
%   publisher sample of chapter 1 was found; metadata (Addison-Wesley,
%   1994, ISBN 0-201-63361-2) from the publisher's catalogue page via
%   WebSearch.  The deck therefore says the rule "formulerades av" the
%   four authors in the book, which the secondary sources agree on, and
%   the user should verify the sentence and page in the book itself.
% COUNTER: session classes-komposition, queries M1 and M2 on openalex,
%   dblp, wos and scopus (see bilaga B): no rival claimant to the rule
%   was found; what the counter-search found is dispute about the
%   rule's CONTENT (the controlled experiments on inheritance and
%   maintainability), cited in the chapter.
% QUOTE (reMarkable, p. 20): "That leads us to our second principle of
%   object-oriented design: / Favor object composition over class
%   inheritance." -- the principle set alone on an indented line, in
%   italics, under the sub-heading "Inheritance versus Composition"
%   (printed p. 18, section 1.6 "How Design Patterns Solve Design
%   Problems", pp. 18--20).  The paragraph after it: "Reuse by inheritance
%   makes it easier to make new components that can be composed with old
%   ones. Inheritance and object composition thus work together.
%   Nevertheless, our experience is that designers overuse inheritance as
%   a reuse technique, and designs are often made more reusable (and
%   simpler) by depending more on object composition."  The other
%   principle, "Program to an interface, not an implementation." (p. 18),
%   is introduced only as "the following principle"; the composition rule
%   is the one the book numbers ("second").
% VERIFIED (reMarkable, 2026-09-04): read in the user's copy on the
%   reMarkable (/Design Patterns Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented
%   Software; printed p. 20 = PDF p. 40, running head "20 INTRODUCTION");
%   the page was also rendered as an image to confirm the italics.
%   Applies because the deck's rule says "föredra", not "undvik": the
%   book's own claim is comparative and experiential ("overuse"), and it
%   says the two mechanisms work together.
% DATE: 2026-09-03
% FOUND-VIA (here): bilaga B, which reported the composition search,
%   moved on 2026-09-22 to modules/classes/slides (Klasser och objekt)
%   as its bilaga D; "bilaga B" above, where it names that chapter,
%   means it.  This deck keeps the entry for the body text that cites
%   it and points to that appendix.
% DATE (here): 2026-09-22
% CLAIM (here, bilaga D): (qualifies) Even the book that made reuse its subtitle says designing
%   reusable OO software is harder than designing OO software, and that
%   novices take long to learn it.
% FOUND-VIA (here, bilaga D): known item, read on the user's reMarkable (/Design Patterns Elements
%   of Reusable Object-Oriented Software), 2026-10-02; the entry already
%   exists in the course (Klasser och objekt, bilaga D, p. 20 verified
%   there 2026-09-04). Not returned by topic search (a book).
% PICKED (here, bilaga D): the book the deck already cites for "komposition före arv"; its
%   opening sentence qualifies the reuse claim from inside the OO canon.
% QUOTE (here, bilaga D, p. 1): "Designing object-oriented software is hard, and designing reusable
%   object-oriented software is even harder." ... "It takes a long time
%   for novices to learn what good object-oriented design is all about."
% VERIFIED (here, bilaga D): read in the user's copy on the reMarkable: printed p. 1 = PDF p. 21
%   (chapter 1 opening; the extracted text shows the folio "1" before the
%   page-22 marker). Metadata as in the course's existing entry.
% COUNTER (here, bilaga D): n/a.
% DATE (here, bilaga D): 2026-10-02
@book{Gamma1994DesignPatterns,
  author    = {Gamma, Erich and Helm, Richard and Johnson, Ralph and
               Vlissides, John},
  title     = {Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented
               Software},
  publisher = {Addison-Wesley},
  address   = {Reading, MA},
